# Pour until a scale says enough has come out, then return the vessel upright.
#
# The controller closes the loop on a weight it reads itself; this goal only
# says how much, and how carefully. Every bound left at 0 falls back to the
# controller's configured value, the same convention Gripper.action uses, so a
# caller that has no opinion about tilt rates does not have to invent one.
float64 target_grams
# Wrist angular velocity ceiling [rad/s]. The pour joint is the one whose axis
# tilts the held vessel, so this is also the vessel's tilt rate.
float64 max_tilt_rate
# Absolute bound on the tilt this pour may reach [rad], measured from the
# attitude the vessel was carried in. It is what stops a pour that the scale
# never satisfies -- an empty vessel, a blocked spout, a scale that reads zero.
float64 max_tilt
# How close to target_grams counts as done [g].
float64 tolerance
# Give up after this long [s], counted from the first commanded tilt.
float64 timeout
---
bool is_completed
# What the scale read when the pour stopped, and how far the vessel had tilted
# at that moment. Both are measurements, not commands: the executed tilt is the
# integrated command, which is what the vessel actually saw.
float64 final_grams
float64 peak_tilt
float64 elapsed
# Empty on success. On failure this says which bound stopped the pour, so an
# operator can tell "tilted as far as allowed and nothing came out" from "the
# scale went quiet".
string message
---
float64 current_grams
float64 tilt
float64 elapsed
